WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.134 --> 00:00:01.770
- Hello, my name is Ben Brown.

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.220
I'm a filmmaker and photographer,

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.214
and we are in the Kruger National Park.

00:00:06.214 --> 00:00:08.549
After meeting my girlfriend
Nicole in South Africa,

00:00:08.549 --> 00:00:10.689
she would often tell
me about the challenges

00:00:10.689 --> 00:00:12.043
facing the rhinos.

00:00:12.043 --> 00:00:15.089
After seeing it for myself
firsthand last year,

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:17.738
I began to understand the
reality of the situation.

00:00:17.738 --> 00:00:19.007
Now we're both here,

00:00:19.007 --> 00:00:23.090
to get the latest updates
on the war on poaching.

00:00:27.311 --> 00:00:30.160
The war on rhino poaching
is not a new topic.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.135
Rhinos have been wiped out
in many African countries

00:00:33.135 --> 00:00:36.039
with the largest population
now living in South Africa,

00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:38.031
in the Kruger National Park.

00:00:38.031 --> 00:00:42.999
In 2014, over 1,200 rhinos
were poached in South Africa.

00:00:42.999 --> 00:00:47.341
The following year, that
number was reduced to 1,175

00:00:47.341 --> 00:00:50.747
thanks to the efforts of
Unite Against Poaching.

00:00:50.747 --> 00:00:54.099
The demand for rhino horn
is at an all time high,

00:00:54.099 --> 00:00:55.653
causing an average of 10 poachers

00:00:55.653 --> 00:00:58.681
to enter the park each day.

00:00:58.681 --> 00:01:01.099
With the number of incidents increasing,

00:01:01.099 --> 00:01:03.470
it's incredible to see
the park gain more control

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:07.637
of the situation and reduce
the number of poached rhino.

00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:10.243
- My name is John Turner,

00:01:10.243 --> 00:01:12.895
National Chairman of Conservation Services

00:01:12.895 --> 00:01:15.962
in the South African National
Parks Honorary Rangers.

00:01:15.962 --> 00:01:19.022
The rhino crisis
changed all our lives, um,

00:01:19.022 --> 00:01:21.192
about seven years ago.

00:01:21.192 --> 00:01:23.478
A rhino poacher is basically for the horn.

00:01:23.478 --> 00:01:24.743
They leave the carcass behind.

00:01:24.743 --> 00:01:26.944
They don't take any meat
or anything like that.

00:01:26.944 --> 00:01:30.577
And, um, contrary to public
opinion, um, the horn

00:01:30.577 --> 00:01:33.940
is 70 percent used for
ornamental purposes, jewelry.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.975
Uh, 30 percent is used
for medicinal purposes.

00:01:36.975 --> 00:01:40.642
And in the East, it's
a great status symbol.

00:01:44.148 --> 00:01:47.570
Rhino horn is now probably
the most expensive, uh,

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.819
commodity on this planet, um,
it's certainly overtaken gold.

00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:52.670
Things like gold and platinum, by far.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.723
They'll end uses, end up paying
up to 95 thousand dollars

00:01:55.723 --> 00:01:58.276
US dollars a kilogram.

00:01:58.276 --> 00:02:00.050
Originally when this crisis hit us,

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:01.755
the first thing we had
to do was re-train all

00:02:01.755 --> 00:02:03.633
our field rangers and re-equip them.

00:02:03.633 --> 00:02:06.095
We started with uh, a couple of dogs, uh,

00:02:06.095 --> 00:02:07.908
all some seven years ago or so.

00:02:07.908 --> 00:02:12.529
And uh, I think the dog
population's grown to about 50 now.

00:02:12.529 --> 00:02:16.145
Our success rate with catching
poachers has improved hugely

00:02:16.145 --> 00:02:18.217
with, with the use of dogs.

00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:21.478
We have tracker dogs,
which follow spur for us.

00:02:21.478 --> 00:02:24.806
We would take days to possibly,
to track and catch a poacher,

00:02:24.806 --> 00:02:27.866
whereas the dogs cutting
that down to hours now.

00:02:27.866 --> 00:02:29.969
We then are looking now,
and working towards,

00:02:29.969 --> 00:02:33.341
getting sniffer dogs in
to work at our gates,

00:02:33.341 --> 00:02:36.079
for sniffing for obviously animal parts,

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:38.650
and also for arms and ammunition.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:42.302
The, the rhino's in a
good state in Kruger.

00:02:42.302 --> 00:02:46.511
Um, in 2015, we’ve managed
to show a reduction

00:02:46.511 --> 00:02:48.614
in the number of animals lost.

00:02:48.614 --> 00:02:51.230
And, um, we're confident
that we can take this on,

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.872
and we will eventually win this war.

00:02:54.872 --> 00:02:56.786
- Okay, we're off to go and find Ben and SP.

00:02:56.786 --> 00:02:58.338
They're hidden somewhere in the bush,

00:02:58.338 --> 00:03:00.611
and we're going to take
the tracker dogs with us.

00:03:00.611 --> 00:03:02.406
We're gonna test out their
skills, and hopefully

00:03:02.406 --> 00:03:03.703
they're gonna snuff them out.

00:03:03.703 --> 00:03:05.370
Let's see! Let's go!

00:03:12.111 --> 00:03:13.231
So how long do you train the dogs

00:03:13.231 --> 00:03:16.273
before they go out into the
wild and track poachers?

00:03:16.273 --> 00:03:18.005
- Every dog, it, it depends.

00:03:18.005 --> 00:03:20.084
Um, but it's average
between six to eight weeks.

00:03:20.084 --> 00:03:21.219
- Okay, and how long has Badger

00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:22.475
been working in the field for?

00:03:22.475 --> 00:03:23.998
- Badger has been working
in the field for two years.

00:03:23.998 --> 00:03:27.398
It is a big far, a big far
area, so when you go there,

00:03:27.398 --> 00:03:29.430
um, there's obviously
always dangerous game there

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.297
you have to be aware of,
poachers are heavily armed,

00:03:32.297 --> 00:03:36.389
um, so it is, it is occupational hazard,
if I can put it like that.

00:03:36.389 --> 00:03:39.381
But, a good dog can work
from the promise for probably about

00:03:39.381 --> 00:03:41.290
three to, what, seven years.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:44.044
Then when the dogs have done
and they're retired, uh,

00:03:44.044 --> 00:03:46.353
they either get the choice to go
with their handler, and

00:03:46.353 --> 00:03:49.353
you can go on pension with your dog.

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:52.329
- We can hear something.

00:03:55.299 --> 00:03:58.784
That's so amazing. That
is absolutely incredible.

00:03:58.784 --> 00:04:00.643
- How's it feel being caught?

00:04:00.643 --> 00:04:02.560
- It feels pretty good.

00:04:05.276 --> 00:04:07.547
It's hard to understand the
lengths these volunteers go

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:11.324
to protect the rhinos, until
you experience it yourself.

00:04:11.324 --> 00:04:13.287
After having the opportunity
to spend some time

00:04:13.287 --> 00:04:15.939
with the volunteers,
staying in their camp,

00:04:15.939 --> 00:04:17.922
you understand the passion
and the love they have for the

00:04:17.922 --> 00:04:22.190
park, the rhinos they protect,
and each of their dogs.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.931
From sniffing out arms and
ammunition, to tracking poachers,

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:28.471
which ultimately leads
to increased arrests,

00:04:28.471 --> 00:04:30.477
it's clear to see these
dogs are playing a huge role

00:04:30.477 --> 00:04:32.810
in the war against poaching.

00:04:34.092 --> 00:04:36.369
Limited funding means efficiency is key

00:04:36.369 --> 00:04:40.625
in this increasingly expensive
fight against the poachers.

00:04:40.625 --> 00:04:42.885
The war is by no means over.

00:04:42.885 --> 00:04:44.363
But there is more hope than ever

00:04:44.363 --> 00:04:46.946
to save this beautiful species.

